{"subjectId":"java","id":"switch-pattern-matching","title":"Switch Expressions & Pattern Matching","group":"Core Language: Strings, Arrays & Methods","order":13,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Modern <code>switch</code> (Java 14) is an expression with arrow labels and no fall-through; Java 16–21 add pattern matching for <code>instanceof</code>, type patterns in <code>switch</code>, guards and record patterns.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-down\"></i>Classic switch statement (fall-through)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int day = 2;\n        switch (day) {\n            case 1:\n                System.out.println(\"Mon\");\n            case 2:\n                System.out.println(\"Tue\");      // matches here...\n            case 3:\n                System.out.println(\"Wed\");      // ...and falls through (no break!)\n                break;\n            default:\n                System.out.println(\"Other\");\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Tue\nWed</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Old-style <code>switch</code> works on <code>int</code>-like types (<code>byte</code>, <code>short</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>int</code> and their wrappers), <code>String</code> (Java 7) and <code>enum</code> — never on <code>long</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>double</code> or <code>boolean</code>.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-right\"></i>Switch expressions (Java 14)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    enum Day { MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI, SAT, SUN }\n\n    static int letters(Day d) {\n        return switch (d) {                     // an expression — yields a value\n            case MON, FRI, SUN -&gt; 6;            // several labels, no fall-through\n            case TUE -&gt; 7;\n            case THU, SAT -&gt; 8;\n            case WED -&gt; {\n                int n = \"Wednesday\".length();\n                yield n;                        // yield returns from a block\n            }\n        };                                      // exhaustive over the enum: no default needed\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(letters(Day.MON) + \" \" + letters(Day.WED));\n        String cmd = \"stop\";\n        String msg = switch (cmd) {\n            case \"go\" -&gt; \"moving\";\n            case \"stop\" -&gt; \"halted\";\n            default -&gt; \"unknown\";\n        };\n        System.out.println(msg);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>6 9\nhalted</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>case X:</code> (colon)</th><th><code>case X -&gt;</code> (arrow)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fall-through</td><td>Yes, until <code>break</code></td><td>Never</td></tr><tr><td>As an expression</td><td>Yes, with <code>yield</code></td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple labels</td><td><code>case 1: case 2:</code></td><td><code>case 1, 2 -&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td>Exhaustiveness</td><td>Only required for switch <em>expressions</em></td><td>Same</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Pattern matching for <code>instanceof</code> (Java 16)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static String describe(Object o) {\n        if (o instanceof String s &amp;&amp; !s.isEmpty())   // test + cast + bind in one step\n            return \"String of length \" + s.length();\n        if (o instanceof Integer i)\n            return \"Integer doubled \" + (i * 2);\n        return \"something else\";\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(describe(\"hello\"));\n        System.out.println(describe(21));\n        System.out.println(describe(3.5));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>String of length 5\nInteger doubled 42\nsomething else</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-filter\"></i>Type patterns, guards &amp; null in switch (Java 21)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static String classify(Object o) {\n        return switch (o) {\n            case null -&gt; \"null!\";                          // otherwise NPE\n            case Integer i when i &lt; 0 -&gt; \"negative int\";   // guard with `when`\n            case Integer i -&gt; \"int \" + i;\n            case String s when s.isBlank() -&gt; \"blank string\";\n            case String s -&gt; \"string '\" + s + \"'\";\n            case int[] arr -&gt; \"int array of \" + arr.length;\n            default -&gt; \"other: \" + o.getClass().getSimpleName();\n        };\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Object[] inputs = {-5, 7, \" \", \"hi\", new int[3], 2.5, null};\n        for (Object o : inputs) System.out.println(classify(o));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>negative int\nint 7\nblank string\nstring 'hi'\nint array of 3\nother: Double\nnull!</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Order matters: a more general pattern before a specific one (<code>case Integer i</code> before <code>case Integer i when i &lt; 0</code>) is a <em>dominance</em> compile error. Without <code>case null</code>, a <code>null</code> selector throws <code>NullPointerException</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Record patterns &amp; sealed hierarchies (Java 21)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rect, Square {}\n    record Circle(double r) implements Shape {}\n    record Rect(double w, double h) implements Shape {}\n    record Square(double side) implements Shape {}\n\n    static double area(Shape s) {\n        return switch (s) {                        // exhaustive: compiler knows all subtypes\n            case Circle(double r) -&gt; Math.PI * r * r;\n            case Rect(double w, double h) -&gt; w * h;     // deconstruct the record\n            case Square(var side) -&gt; side * side;\n        };\n    }\n\n    record Point(int x, int y) {}\n    record Line(Point from, Point to) {}\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.printf(\"%.2f %.1f %.1f%n\", area(new Circle(1)), area(new Rect(2, 3)), area(new Square(4)));\n        Object o = new Line(new Point(0, 0), new Point(3, 4));\n        if (o instanceof Line(Point(var x1, var y1), Point(var x2, var y2)))   // nested patterns\n            System.out.println(\"dx=\" + (x2 - x1) + \" dy=\" + (y2 - y1));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3.14 6.0 16.0\ndx=3 dy=4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Why it matters</div><p>Sealed interface + records + pattern <code>switch</code> = algebraic data types in Java. Add a new subtype and every non-exhaustive <code>switch</code> fails to compile — the compiler finds the code you forgot.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does yield do in a switch expression?</summary><div><p>It returns a value from a block-bodied <code>case</code> (<code>case X -&gt; { …; yield v; }</code>) or from a colon-style case in a switch <em>expression</em>. <code>return</code> would try to leave the enclosing method instead.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is a default needed in a switch expression?</summary><div><p>The switch expression must be <strong>exhaustive</strong>. For an <code>enum</code> or a <code>sealed</code> type covering every constant/subtype, no <code>default</code> is needed; for <code>int</code>, <code>String</code> or <code>Object</code> you need <code>default</code> (or an unconditional pattern).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What happens if the selector of a switch is null?</summary><div><p>Traditionally a <code>NullPointerException</code>. From Java 21 you can add <code>case null</code> (or <code>case null, default -&gt;</code>) to handle it explicitly.</p></div></details></section>"}